WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.769
Your checkout service sends a payment to a card issuer, across a router you don't own.

2
00:00:07.769 --> 00:00:10.009
What stops the router from reading it?

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:10.876
Nothing.

4
00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:17.125
It reads every byte, and it can change 100 to 900 and pass the request on.

5
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:19.667
Encryption needs a key both ends share.

6
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.659
Sending it doesn't work: the router would copy it.

7
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:27.180
So each side keeps a secret, and sends only a number made from it.

8
00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.755
The client keeps 6, and sends 8 in its first message, the ClientHello.

9
00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:35.993
The issuer keeps 15, and answers 19 in its ServerHello.

10
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:41.100
Each side mixes the other's number with its own secret, and both get 2.

11
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.757
The router saw 8 and 19.

12
00:00:42.757 --> 00:00:47.036
With real 32-byte numbers, it can't get from those to the key.

13
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.779
That took one round trip.

14
00:00:48.779 --> 00:00:52.568
Everything after the ServerHello is encrypted with that key.

15
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:58.764
But the router could play both parts, with one exchange on each side, passing messages between them.

16
00:00:58.764 --> 00:01:01.463
You'd have an encrypted line to the attacker.

17
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:03.941
So the issuer proves who it is.

18
00:01:03.941 --> 00:01:11.311
It sends a certificate: this public key belongs to api.pay.test, signed by a certificate authority.

19
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.247
The client walks up the chain.

20
00:01:13.247 --> 00:01:19.129
The authority's certificate came along, signed by a root that is already in the client's trust store.

21
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.107
Anyone can copy a certificate.

22
00:01:21.107 --> 00:01:26.951
So the issuer also signs the whole handshake with its private key, which never leaves the issuer.

23
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:30.837
An attacker in the router has the certificate, but not that key.

24
00:01:30.837 --> 00:01:35.061
It can't make the signature, so the check fails, and the client hangs up.

25
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:38.631
Every record is now encrypted, with a 16-byte tag.

26
00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:41.689
Flip one bit on the way, and the check fails.

27
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:47.812
The router still sees addresses, record sizes and the server name, and it can drop packets.

28
00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:49.837
It can't read or change a byte.

29
00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:53.763
One round trip agrees a key nobody on the path can compute.

30
00:01:53.763 --> 00:01:57.492
A chain to a root you trust proves who holds the other end.
